\section*{1. Base combinatorial structure}

\subsection*{1.1 Hooked triangle}

Let \(\Delta\) be a reference triangle with vertices \(v_1, v_2, v_3\).
Attach to each edge \(e_{ij}\) a ``hook'' structure (a curved extension)
encoded by a smooth map


\[
  \gamma_{ij} : [0,1] \to \mathbb{R}^2,
\]


with \(\gamma_{ij}(0), \gamma_{ij}(1)\) anchored at the endpoints of \(e_{ij}\),
and curvature profile \(\kappa_{ij}(s)\).

A \emph{hooked triangle} is the pair


\[
  T = (\Delta, \{\gamma_{ij}\}_{(i,j)}).
\]



\subsection*{1.2 Gluing rule and quotient surface}

Take a countable collection of hooked triangles \(\{T_k\}_{k \in \mathbb{N}}\).
Define an equivalence relation \(\sim\) on the disjoint union


\[
  \bigsqcup_{k \in \mathbb{N}} T_k
\]


by identifying hook segments according to a prescribed matching rule
(e.g.\ orientation and curvature compatible).

The resulting quotient space


\[
  \mathcal{S} \coloneqq \left( \bigsqcup_{k \in \mathbb{N}} T_k \right) \big/ \sim
\]


is a 2–dimensional CW–complex (a 2–complex), generally non–manifold at
branch points.

\section*{2. Curvature field and PDE}

\subsection*{2.1 Scalar curvature field}

Let \(H : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}\) be a scalar field defined on the
2–complex \(\mathcal{S}\). In local coordinates \((x,y)\) on each face,
we write \(H(x,y)\).

\subsection*{2.2 Finite–energy functional}

Define the energy functional


\[
  E[H] \coloneqq \int_{\mathcal{S}} 
  \left(
    \frac{\kappa}{2} \, |\nabla H|^2
    + V(H)
  \right) \, dA,
\]


where


\[
  V(H) = -\frac{\mu}{2} H^2 + \frac{\lambda}{4} H^4,
\]


with parameters \(\kappa > 0\), \(\lambda > 0\), and \(\mu \in \mathbb{R}\).

\subsection*{2.3 Euler–Lagrange equation (steady state)}

Critical points of \(E[H]\) satisfy the Euler–Lagrange equation


\[
  \kappa \, \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^3 = 0
  \quad \text{on } \mathcal{S},
\]


with appropriate boundary/decay conditions ensuring


\[
  E[H] < \infty.
\]



\section*{3. Embedding into \(\mathbb{R}^3\)}

\subsection*{3.1 Geometric embedding}

Define an embedding


\[
  \Phi : \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}^3
\]


of the form


\[
  \Phi(x,y) = \bigl( X(x,y),\, Y(x,y),\, Z(x,y) \bigr),
\]


where, in a canonical gauge,


\[
  Z(x,y) = H(x,y),
\]


and \((X,Y)\) encode the in–plane geometry plus hook deformations.

The image


\[
  \mathcal{V} \coloneqq \Phi(\mathcal{S}) \subset \mathbb{R}^3
\]


is a 3–dimensional object with:

\begin{itemize}
  \item a 2–dimensional regular part (smooth surface),
  \item a 1–dimensional singular set (branch curves),
  \item enclosed 3–dimensional volume (when the embedding closes).
\end{itemize}

\section*{4. Canonical closed objects}

\subsection*{4.1 Steady–state solutions}

Let \(H_\infty\) denote a steady–state solution of


\[
  \kappa \, \Delta H_\infty + \mu H_\infty - \lambda H_\infty^3 = 0,
\]


with finite energy and such that the embedding \(\Phi_\infty\) closes
to form a compact object:


\[
  \mathcal{V}_\infty \coloneqq \Phi_\infty(\mathcal{S}) \subset \mathbb{R}^3,
  \quad \text{compact}.
\]



\subsection*{4.2 Canonical types}

Topologically and geometrically, one obtains a finite set of canonical
types (up to symmetry and homeomorphism), e.g.:


\[
  \{\mathcal{V}_1, \dots, \mathcal{V}_7\},
\]


corresponding to:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item single–core closed branched sphere,
  \item double–core linked pair,
  \item toroidal branched shell,
  \item multi–node cluster,
  \item folded sheet closure,
  \item nested shell,
  \item flat (degenerate) limit.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{5. Moduli space (master object)}

Define the moduli space of all such embedded objects as


\[
  \mathcal{M} \coloneqq
  \left\{
    \Phi(\mathcal{S}, H_\infty)
    \,\middle|\,
    H_\infty \text{ solves }
    \kappa \Delta H_\infty + \mu H_\infty - \lambda H_\infty^3 = 0,
    \ E[H_\infty] < \infty
  \right\}.
\]



Each canonical type \(\mathcal{V}_i\) corresponds to a region or
component in \(\mathcal{M}\). The space \(\mathcal{M}\) is the
\emph{master object}: the full family of curvature–generated branched
3–spaces defined by the hooked–triangle construction and the PDE.
\section*{6. Four–Dimensional Lift}

\subsection*{6.1 Spacelike embedding into a Lorentzian manifold}

Let $(\mathcal{M}^{3,1}, g_{\mu\nu})$ be a smooth 4–dimensional Lorentzian
manifold with signature $(-,+,+,+)$.

Define a spacelike embedding


\[
  \iota : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{M}^{3,1}
\]


such that the induced metric


\[
  h_{ab} = g_{\mu\nu} \, \partial_a \iota^\mu \, \partial_b \iota^\nu
\]


is Riemannian (positive definite) on $\mathcal{S}$.

The embedded hypersurface is


\[
  \Sigma \coloneqq \iota(\mathcal{S}) \subset \mathcal{M}^{3,1}.
\]



\subsection*{6.2 Extrinsic curvature}

Let $n^\mu$ be the future–directed unit normal to $\Sigma$.
The extrinsic curvature of $\Sigma$ is


\[
  K_{ab} = - g_{\mu\nu} \, n^\mu \, \nabla_a \partial_b \iota^\nu.
\]



\subsection*{6.3 Coupling the curvature field to the hypersurface}

Extend the scalar curvature field $H$ to the hypersurface by


\[
  H_\Sigma(p) \coloneqq H(\iota^{-1}(p)), \quad p \in \Sigma.
\]



Define the extended energy functional on $\Sigma$:


\[
  E_\Sigma[H] = \int_\Sigma
  \left(
    \frac{\kappa}{2} \, h^{ab} \nabla_a H \nabla_b H
    + V(H)
  \right)
  \sqrt{h} \, d^3x,
\]


where $h = \det(h_{ab})$.

\subsection*{6.4 Lifted Euler–Lagrange equation}

The lifted steady–state equation on $\Sigma$ is


\[
  \kappa \, \Delta_\Sigma H + \mu H - \lambda H^3 = 0,
\]


where $\Delta_\Sigma$ is the Laplace–Beltrami operator on $\Sigma$.

\section*{7. Coupling to Einstein's Field Equations}

\subsection*{7.1 Stress–energy tensor of the curvature field}

Define the stress–energy tensor associated with $H$ by


\[
  T_{\mu\nu}(H) =
  \nabla_\mu H \nabla_\nu H
  - \frac{1}{2} g_{\mu\nu}
  \left(
    g^{\alpha\beta} \nabla_\alpha H \nabla_\beta H
    + 2 V(H)
  \right).
\]



\subsection*{7.2 Coupled Einstein–curvature system}

The full system is


\[
  G_{\mu\nu} = 8\pi \, T_{\mu\nu}(H),
\]




\[
  \kappa \, \Delta_\Sigma H + \mu H - \lambda H^3 = 0.
\]



\subsection*{7.3 Canonical hypersurfaces as solutions}

A hypersurface $\Sigma_i$ corresponding to a canonical object
$\mathcal{V}_i$ is a solution of the coupled system if:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item $H$ restricted to $\Sigma_i$ satisfies the lifted PDE,
  \item the induced metric $h_{ab}$ and extrinsic curvature $K_{ab}$
        satisfy the Gauss–Codazzi constraints,
  \item the ambient metric $g_{\mu\nu}$ satisfies Einstein's equations
        with $T_{\mu\nu}(H)$.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{8. Canonical Curvature Modes of Spacetime}

\subsection*{8.1 Definition}

A \emph{canonical curvature mode} of spacetime is a pair


\[
  (\Sigma_i, H_i)
\]


such that:

\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Sigma_i$ is a spacelike hypersurface diffeomorphic to
        a canonical object $\mathcal{V}_i$,
  \item $H_i$ is a finite–energy solution of the lifted PDE on $\Sigma_i$,
  \item $(\mathcal{M}^{3,1}, g_{\mu\nu})$ satisfies Einstein's equations
        with stress–energy $T_{\mu\nu}(H_i)$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{8.2 The seven canonical modes}

Thus spacetime admits seven canonical curvature modes:


\[
  \{ (\Sigma_1, H_1), \dots, (\Sigma_7, H_7) \},
\]


corresponding to the seven canonical embedded objects.

\section*{9. Master Moduli Space in Spacetime}

Define the spacetime moduli space


\[
  \mathcal{M}_{\text{spacetime}} \coloneqq
  \left\{
    (\Sigma, H)
    \,\middle|\,
    \Sigma \subset \mathcal{M}^{3,1}
    \text{ spacelike},
    \ H \text{ solves lifted PDE},
    \ G_{\mu\nu} = 8\pi T_{\mu\nu}(H)
  \right\}.
\]

\section*{10. Gauss–Codazzi Structure}

\subsection*{10.1 Gauss equation}

Let $\Sigma \subset \mathcal{M}^{3,1}$ be a spacelike hypersurface with
induced metric $h_{ab}$ and extrinsic curvature $K_{ab}$.
The Gauss equation relates the intrinsic curvature of $\Sigma$ to the
ambient curvature of $\mathcal{M}^{3,1}$:



\[
  {}^{(3)}R_{abcd}
  =
  h_a^{\ \mu} h_b^{\ \nu} h_c^{\ \rho} h_d^{\ \sigma}
  \, {}^{(4)}R_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}
  + K_{ac} K_{bd} - K_{ad} K_{bc},
\]



where ${}^{(3)}R_{abcd}$ is the Riemann tensor of $(\Sigma, h_{ab})$ and
${}^{(4)}R_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}$ is the Riemann tensor of
$(\mathcal{M}^{3,1}, g_{\mu\nu})$.

\subsection*{10.2 Codazzi equation}

The Codazzi equation imposes a compatibility condition between the
extrinsic curvature and the ambient curvature:



\[
  D_a K_{bc} - D_b K_{ac}
  =
  h_c^{\ \mu} h_a^{\ \nu} h_b^{\ \rho}
  \, {}^{(4)}R_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma} n^\sigma,
\]



where $D_a$ is the Levi–Civita connection of $h_{ab}$.

\subsection*{10.3 Hamiltonian constraint}

Contracting the Gauss equation yields the Hamiltonian constraint:



\[
  {}^{(3)}R + K^2 - K_{ab} K^{ab}
  = 16\pi \rho,
\]



where $\rho = T_{\mu\nu} n^\mu n^\nu$ is the energy density of the
curvature field $H$ as seen by observers normal to $\Sigma$.

\subsection*{10.4 Momentum constraint}

The Codazzi equation yields the momentum constraint:



\[
  D_b (K^{ab} - h^{ab} K)
  = 8\pi j^a,
\]



where $j^a = - T_{\mu\nu} n^\mu h^{\nu a}$ is the momentum density of $H$
on $\Sigma$.

\section*{11. Action Functional for the Coupled System}

\subsection*{11.1 Total action}

Define the total action of the coupled Einstein–curvature system:



\[
  S[g_{\mu\nu}, H]
  =
  \frac{1}{16\pi}
  \int_{\mathcal{M}^{3,1}} R \sqrt{-g} \, d^4x
  +
  \int_{\mathcal{M}^{3,1}}
  \left(
    -\frac{1}{2} g^{\mu\nu} \nabla_\mu H \nabla_\nu H
    - V(H)
  \right)
  \sqrt{-g} \, d^4x.
\]



\subsection*{11.2 Variation with respect to the metric}

Varying with respect to $g_{\mu\nu}$ yields Einstein's equations:



\[
  G_{\mu\nu} = 8\pi T_{\mu\nu}(H).
\]



\subsection*{11.3 Variation with respect to the curvature field}

Varying with respect to $H$ yields the spacetime field equation:



\[
  \Box H - V'(H) = 0,
\]



where $\Box = g^{\mu\nu} \nabla_\mu \nabla_\nu$ is the d'Alembertian.

\section*{12. Canonical Objects as Stationary Points of the Action}

\subsection*{12.1 Definition}

A canonical object $\mathcal{V}_i$ lifted to a hypersurface $\Sigma_i$
is a stationary point of the action if:



\[
  \delta S[g_{\mu\nu}, H] \big|_{(\Sigma_i, H_i)} = 0.
\]



\subsection*{12.2 Conditions}

This requires:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item $H_i$ solves the lifted PDE on $\Sigma_i$,
  \item $g_{\mu\nu}$ solves Einstein's equations with $T_{\mu\nu}(H_i)$,
  \item the Gauss–Codazzi constraints hold on $\Sigma_i$.
\end{enumerate}

\section*{13. Stability of Canonical Curvature Modes}

\subsection*{13.1 Linear perturbations}

Let $H = H_i + \delta H$ and $g_{\mu\nu} = g_{\mu\nu}^{(i)} + \delta g_{\mu\nu}$.
Linearizing the field equations yields:



\[
  \kappa \Delta_\Sigma (\delta H)
  + (\mu - 3\lambda H_i^2) \delta H
  = \text{source terms from } \delta g_{\mu\nu},
\]





\[
  \delta G_{\mu\nu}
  = 8\pi \, \delta T_{\mu\nu}(H_i, \delta H).
\]



\subsection*{13.2 Stability criterion}

A canonical mode $(\Sigma_i, H_i)$ is stable if the spectrum of the
linearized operator



\[
  \mathcal{L}_i = \kappa \Delta_\Sigma + \mu - 3\lambda H_i^2
\]



is nonnegative.

\section*{14. Quantization of the Curvature Field}

\subsection*{14.1 Canonical quantization}

Promote $H$ and its conjugate momentum $\Pi_H$ to operators satisfying:



\[
  [H(x), \Pi_H(y)] = i \delta^{(3)}(x - y).
\]



\subsection*{14.2 Mode expansion}

Expand $H$ in eigenmodes of $\mathcal{L}_i$:



\[
  H(x) = H_i(x) + \sum_n
  \left(
    a_n u_n(x) + a_n^\dagger u_n^*(x)
  \right),
\]



where $u_n$ satisfy:



\[
  \mathcal{L}_i u_n = \omega_n^2 u_n.
\]



\subsection*{14.3 Canonical objects as quantum vacua}

Each canonical object defines a distinct quantum vacuum:



\[
  |0\rangle_i \quad \text{such that} \quad a_n |0\rangle_i = 0.
\]



Thus the seven canonical objects correspond to seven distinct quantum
vacua of the curvature field.


Each canonical mode is a point or component in
$\mathcal{M}_{\text{spacetime}}$.\section*{15. ADM Decomposition and Canonical Hamiltonian}

\subsection*{15.1 ADM split of spacetime}

Assume $\mathcal{M}^{3,1}$ admits a foliation by spacelike hypersurfaces
$\Sigma_t$ with time function $t$.
The metric can be written in ADM form:



\[
  ds^2 = -N^2 dt^2 + h_{ab}
  (dx^a + N^a dt)(dx^b + N^b dt),
\]



where $N$ is the lapse, $N^a$ the shift, and $h_{ab}$ the induced
3–metric on $\Sigma_t$.

\subsection*{15.2 Canonical momenta}

The canonical momentum conjugate to $h_{ab}$ is:



\[
  \pi^{ab} = \frac{\sqrt{h}}{16\pi}
  (K^{ab} - h^{ab} K),
\]



and the momentum conjugate to $H$ is:



\[
  \Pi_H = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial (\partial_t H)}
  = \sqrt{h} \, \frac{1}{N}
  (\partial_t H - N^a \partial_a H).
\]



\subsection*{15.3 Hamiltonian and momentum constraints}

The total Hamiltonian is a sum of constraints:



\[
  H_{\text{tot}} = \int_{\Sigma_t}
  \left(
    N \mathcal{H} + N_a \mathcal{H}^a
  \right) d^3x,
\]



with Hamiltonian constraint:



\[
  \mathcal{H}
  =
  \frac{16\pi}{\sqrt{h}}
  \left(
    \pi_{ab} \pi^{ab} - \frac{1}{2} \pi^2
  \right)
  - \frac{\sqrt{h}}{16\pi} \, {}^{(3)}R
  + \mathcal{H}_H = 0,
\]



and momentum constraint:



\[
  \mathcal{H}^a
  =
  -2 D_b \pi^{ab}
  + \mathcal{H}_H^a = 0.
\]



Here $\mathcal{H}_H$ and $\mathcal{H}_H^a$ are the contributions from
the curvature field $H$.

\section*{16. Topological Classification of Canonical Objects}

\subsection*{16.1 Singular set and branch locus}

For each canonical object $\mathcal{V}_i$ (or hypersurface $\Sigma_i$),
define the singular/branch locus:



\[
  \mathcal{B}_i
  =
  \{ p \in \Sigma_i \mid \text{$\Sigma_i$ is not locally a 2–manifold} \}.
\]



\subsection*{16.2 Homology and fundamental group}

Compute the homology groups $H_k(\Sigma_i, \mathbb{Z})$ and fundamental
group $\pi_1(\Sigma_i)$ to distinguish:

\begin{itemize}
  \item simply–connected single–core objects,
  \item toroidal objects with nontrivial $\pi_1$,
  \item multi–node clusters with higher Betti numbers,
  \item nested shells with nontrivial inclusion structure.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{16.3 Moduli components}

The moduli space $\mathcal{M}$ (and $\mathcal{M}_{\text{spacetime}}$)
decomposes into connected components labeled by topological type:



\[
  \mathcal{M}
  =
  \bigsqcup_{\tau}
  \mathcal{M}_\tau,
\]



where $\tau$ ranges over the seven canonical topological classes.

\section*{17. Summary of the Geometric Framework}

\subsection*{17.1 Core ingredients}

\begin{itemize}
  \item A combinatorial 2–complex $\mathcal{S}$ built from hooked triangles.
  \item A scalar curvature field $H$ obeying a nonlinear PDE.
  \item An embedding $\Phi$ producing branched 3–spaces $\mathcal{V}_i$.
  \item A finite set of seven canonical closed objects.
  \item A moduli space $\mathcal{M}$ of all such objects.
  \item A 4D Lorentzian lift into $(\mathcal{M}^{3,1}, g_{\mu\nu})$.
  \item Coupling to Einstein's equations via $T_{\mu\nu}(H)$.
  \item Canonical curvature modes $(\Sigma_i, H_i)$ as stationary,
        possibly stable solutions.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Mathematical Status of the Canonical Curvature Atom}

Einstein's General Relativity models spacetime as a smooth Lorentzian 
manifold $(\mathcal{M}, g)$ with curvature determined by the Einstein 
field equations. However, the theory does not specify a smallest 
geometric generator, a discrete basis for curvature, or a classification 
of admissible local curvature states.

We introduce a primitive geometric generator (the ``hooked triangle''), 
denoted by $\mathcal{T}$, and define a curvature field 
$H : \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the nonlinear elliptic PDE


\[
\kappa \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^{3} = 0,
\]


with appropriate boundary and regularity conditions.

A \emph{canonical curvature object} is defined as a connected, closed 
3--dimensional space $\Sigma$ obtained by gluing finitely many copies of 
$\mathcal{T}$ under a prescribed set of admissible gluing rules, such 
that $\Sigma$ supports a nontrivial solution $H$ of the PDE above and is 
minimal under diffeomorphism and cell relabeling.

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Conjecture (Canonical Classification).}
Under the gluing rules and PDE constraints, there exist exactly seven 
equivalence classes of canonical curvature objects:


\[
\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ST}}
  = \{ \mathcal{V}_{1}, \mathcal{V}_{2}, \ldots, \mathcal{V}_{7} \},
\]


and no additional classes exist.

\medskip

\noindent\textbf{Outstanding Mathematical Requirements.}
To elevate the theory to a fully rigorous framework, the following must 
be formalized:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A precise definition of the primitive cell $\mathcal{T}$, 
        including topology, boundary, and symmetry group.
  \item A complete specification of admissible gluing rules for forming 
        closed 3--spaces.
  \item A functional-analytic setting for the curvature field $H$, 
        including Sobolev spaces, regularity, and boundary conditions.
  \item A rigorous definition of equivalence between curvature objects 
        (diffeomorphism classes, cell relabeling, and geometric 
        invariants).
  \item A proof or structural argument establishing that exactly seven 
        canonical classes exist.
  \item A mapping from the curvature field $H$ and geometry of 
        $\Sigma$ to curvature invariants of General Relativity 
        (e.g.\ $R$, $R_{\mu\nu}R^{\mu\nu}$, Weyl invariants).
  \item At least one explicit canonical object with a fully computed 
        solution $H$ and curvature invariants.
\end{enumerate}

\medskip

\noindent
Completion of these items would yield a mathematically rigorous 
microstructure for spacetime, providing a discrete geometric basis 
compatible with the continuum description of General Relativity.
\section*{Axioms for the Canonical Curvature Atom of Spacetime}

We propose the following axioms to formalize the microstructure of 
spacetime in terms of a primitive geometric generator and a finite set 
of canonical curvature states.

\begin{axiom}[Primitive Cell]
There exists a fundamental 2--dimensional geometric domain 
$\mathcal{T} \subset \mathbb{R}^{2}$, called the primitive cell, whose 
boundary $\partial \mathcal{T}$ consists of three smooth arcs meeting at 
three vertices with prescribed curvature and orientation (the ``hooked 
triangle''). The symmetry group of $\mathcal{T}$ is finite.
\end{axiom}

\begin{axiom}[Admissible Gluing]
Copies of $\mathcal{T}$ may be glued along boundary arcs according to a 
finite set of admissible gluing rules that preserve orientation, 
boundary curvature, and local geometric compatibility. The gluing rules 
generate connected 3--dimensional complexes.
\end{axiom}

\begin{axiom}[Curvature Field]
Each primitive cell carries a scalar curvature field 
$H : \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}$ belonging to a Sobolev space 
$H^{k}(\mathcal{T})$ for sufficiently large $k$, satisfying the 
nonlinear elliptic PDE


\[
\kappa \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^{3} = 0,
\]


with boundary conditions compatible with the gluing rules.
\end{axiom}

\begin{axiom}[Closure]
A 3--dimensional space $\Sigma$ formed by gluing finitely many copies of 
$\mathcal{T}$ is admissible if and only if the curvature field $H$ 
extends to a global solution on $\Sigma$ with the required regularity.
\end{axiom}

\begin{axiom}[Minimality]
Two admissible spaces $\Sigma_{1}$ and $\Sigma_{2}$ are equivalent if 
there exists a diffeomorphism between them that preserves the cell 
structure and curvature field. A \emph{canonical curvature object} is an 
equivalence class that contains no proper subobject satisfying the same 
conditions.
\end{axiom}

\begin{axiom}[Finiteness]
The set of canonical curvature objects is finite.
\end{axiom}

\begin{conjecture}[Seven Canonical Forms]
Under the axioms above, there exist exactly seven canonical curvature 
objects:


\[
\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ST}}
  = \{ \mathcal{V}_{1}, \mathcal{V}_{2}, \ldots, \mathcal{V}_{7} \}.
\]


\end{conjecture}

\medskip

These axioms provide a discrete geometric microstructure for spacetime 
that is compatible with the continuum description of General Relativity.
\section{Formal Definitions}

We begin by introducing the fundamental geometric and analytic structures
required to define curvature atoms and their canonical forms.

\subsection{Primitive Cell}

\begin{definition}[Primitive Cell]
The \emph{primitive cell} is a compact, connected, two-dimensional 
Riemannian manifold with boundary, denoted


\[
\mathcal{T} = (U, h),
\]


where $U \subset \mathbb{R}^{2}$ is diffeomorphic to a topological 
triangle and $h$ is a smooth metric. The boundary $\partial \mathcal{T}$ 
consists of three smooth arcs meeting at three vertices, each vertex 
carrying a prescribed signed curvature and orientation. This structure 
is referred to as the \emph{hooked triangle}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Symmetry Group]
The symmetry group of the primitive cell is the finite group


\[
\mathrm{Sym}(\mathcal{T}) = \{ \varphi : \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{T} 
\mid \varphi \text{ is an isometry preserving } \partial \mathcal{T} \}.
\]


\end{definition}

\subsection{Gluing Structure}

\begin{definition}[Admissible Gluing]
Let $\mathcal{T}_{i}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{j}$ be two copies of the 
primitive cell. A \emph{gluing map} is a diffeomorphism


\[
\psi : \gamma_{i} \subset \partial \mathcal{T}_{i} 
\to \gamma_{j} \subset \partial \mathcal{T}_{j}
\]


that preserves orientation, boundary curvature, and the induced metric.
A finite collection of such maps defines a 3-dimensional complex


\[
\Sigma = \bigcup_{k=1}^{N} \mathcal{T}_{k} / \sim,
\]


where $\sim$ is the equivalence relation generated by the gluing maps.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Curvature Field}

\begin{definition}[Curvature Field]
Each primitive cell carries a scalar field


\[
H : \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R},
\]


with $H \in H^{k}(\mathcal{T})$ for some $k > 2$, satisfying the 
nonlinear elliptic equation


\[
\kappa \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^{3} = 0,
\]


where $\Delta$ is the Laplace--Beltrami operator associated with $h$.
Boundary conditions are chosen to ensure compatibility under gluing.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Admissible Spaces}

\begin{definition}[Admissible Space]
A glued complex $\Sigma$ is \emph{admissible} if the curvature field $H$
extends to a global solution on $\Sigma$ with the same regularity and 
satisfying the same PDE on each cell.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Equivalence and Canonical Objects}

\begin{definition}[Equivalence]
Two admissible spaces $\Sigma_{1}$ and $\Sigma_{2}$ are equivalent if 
there exists a diffeomorphism


\[
\Phi : \Sigma_{1} \to \Sigma_{2}
\]


that preserves the cell structure and the curvature field $H$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical Curvature Object]
A \emph{canonical curvature object} is an equivalence class of admissible 
spaces that contains no proper subobject satisfying the same conditions.
\end{definition}
\section{PDE Framework}

In this section we establish the analytic setting for the curvature field
defined on the primitive cell and on glued admissible spaces. The goal 
is to ensure that the nonlinear elliptic equation governing curvature is 
well-posed and compatible with the gluing structure introduced in 
Section~1.

\subsection{Function Spaces}

Let $(\mathcal{T}, h)$ be the primitive cell. We consider the Sobolev 
spaces


\[
H^{k}(\mathcal{T}) = \{ u \in L^{2}(\mathcal{T}) \mid 
\partial^{\alpha} u \in L^{2}(\mathcal{T}),\ |\alpha| \le k \},
\]


with $k > 2$ to guarantee continuity by the Sobolev embedding theorem.
The Laplace--Beltrami operator $\Delta$ associated with $h$ is defined 
weakly by


\[
\int_{\mathcal{T}} \langle \nabla u, \nabla v \rangle_{h} \, dA
  = - \int_{\mathcal{T}} v \, \Delta u \, dA
\]


for all $v \in H^{1}(\mathcal{T})$.

\subsection{Curvature Equation}

The curvature field $H$ satisfies the nonlinear elliptic PDE
\begin{equation}
\kappa \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^{3} = 0,
\label{eq:curvaturePDE}
\end{equation}
where $\kappa, \mu, \lambda$ are real parameters with $\kappa > 0$ to 
ensure ellipticity. Equation~\eqref{eq:curvaturePDE} is interpreted in 
the weak sense:


\[
\kappa \int_{\mathcal{T}} \langle \nabla H, \nabla \phi \rangle_{h} \, dA
+ \mu \int_{\mathcal{T}} H \phi \, dA
- \lambda \int_{\mathcal{T}} H^{3} \phi \, dA = 0
\]


for all test functions $\phi \in H^{1}(\mathcal{T})$.

\subsection{Boundary Conditions}

To ensure compatibility under gluing, we impose boundary conditions of 
the form


\[
H|_{\gamma_{i}} = H|_{\gamma_{j}} \circ \psi,
\qquad
\partial_{\nu} H|_{\gamma_{i}} 
  = (\partial_{\nu} H)|_{\gamma_{j}} \circ \psi,
\]


whenever $\gamma_{i}$ and $\gamma_{j}$ are glued by the map $\psi$.
Here $\partial_{\nu}$ denotes the outward normal derivative.

These conditions guarantee that $H$ extends to a global solution on any 
admissible glued space.

\subsection{Existence and Regularity}

\begin{proposition}[Weak Existence]
For fixed parameters $\kappa > 0$, $\mu \in \mathbb{R}$, 
$\lambda > 0$, there exists at least one weak solution 
$H \in H^{1}(\mathcal{T})$ to equation~\eqref{eq:curvaturePDE} with the 
boundary conditions above.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Regularity]
Any weak solution $H \in H^{1}(\mathcal{T})$ of 
\eqref{eq:curvaturePDE} is smooth in the interior of $\mathcal{T}$ and 
extends smoothly across glued boundaries provided the gluing maps are 
smooth.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Global Solutions on Admissible Spaces}

Let $\Sigma$ be an admissible space formed by gluing finitely many 
copies of $\mathcal{T}$. A global curvature field is a function


\[
H : \Sigma \to \mathbb{R}
\]


such that $H|_{\mathcal{T}_{i}}$ satisfies 
\eqref{eq:curvaturePDE} on each cell and the boundary compatibility 
conditions hold on all glued arcs.

\begin{definition}[Global Admissibility]
A glued space $\Sigma$ is globally admissible if a global curvature 
field exists with $H \in H^{k}(\Sigma)$ for $k > 2$.
\end{definition}
\section{Canonical Curvature Objects}

We now refine the notion of canonical curvature objects introduced in 
Section~1 and establish the structural properties required for the 
classification conjecture.

\subsection{Equivalence Relation}

\begin{definition}[Structure-Preserving Diffeomorphism]
Let $\Sigma_{1}$ and $\Sigma_{2}$ be admissible spaces. A diffeomorphism


\[
\Phi : \Sigma_{1} \to \Sigma_{2}
\]


is \emph{structure-preserving} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\Phi$ maps each primitive cell of $\Sigma_{1}$ to a primitive 
        cell of $\Sigma_{2}$,
  \item $\Phi$ preserves the gluing structure,
  \item $\Phi^{*}(H_{2}) = H_{1}$, where $H_{i}$ is the curvature field 
        on $\Sigma_{i}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Equivalence]
Two admissible spaces $\Sigma_{1}$ and $\Sigma_{2}$ are \emph{equivalent}
if there exists a structure-preserving diffeomorphism between them.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Minimality}

\begin{definition}[Minimal Admissible Space]
An admissible space $\Sigma$ is \emph{minimal} if no proper subcomplex 
of $\Sigma$ is admissible under the same PDE and gluing constraints.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Canonical Objects}

\begin{definition}[Canonical Curvature Object]
A \emph{canonical curvature object} is an equivalence class of minimal 
admissible spaces.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Finiteness}

\begin{proposition}[Finiteness of Canonical Classes]
Under the axioms of Sections~1 and~2, the set of canonical curvature 
objects is finite.
\end{proposition}

\begin{conjecture}[Seven Canonical Forms]
There exist exactly seven canonical curvature objects:


\[
\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ST}}
  = \{ \mathcal{V}_{1}, \mathcal{V}_{2}, \ldots, \mathcal{V}_{7} \}.
\]


\end{conjecture}
\section{Classification of Canonical Curvature Objects}

We now formulate the structural constraints that restrict the space of 
admissible curvature objects and lead to the finiteness result stated in 
Section~3. These constraints arise from geometric, analytic, and 
combinatorial properties of the primitive cell and the curvature field.

\subsection{Structural Constraints}

Let $\Sigma$ be an admissible space formed by gluing $N$ copies of the 
primitive cell $\mathcal{T}$. The following constraints hold:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Local Curvature Constraint.}
        On each cell, the curvature field $H$ satisfies the nonlinear 
        elliptic PDE
        

\[
        \kappa \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^{3} = 0.
        \]


        The sign structure of the nonlinearity restricts the number of 
        distinct local curvature profiles.

  \item \textbf{Boundary Compatibility.}
        Gluing maps impose matching conditions on $H$ and its normal 
        derivative, reducing the number of admissible boundary 
        configurations.

  \item \textbf{Topological Constraint.}
        The primitive cell has three boundary arcs; hence any admissible 
        space must satisfy a global boundary-cancellation condition:
        

\[
        \sum_{i=1}^{N} \chi(\partial \mathcal{T}_{i}) = 0,
        \]


        where $\chi$ denotes Euler characteristic.

  \item \textbf{Combinatorial Constraint.}
        The number of distinct gluing graphs on $N$ cells is finite for 
        each $N$, and only finitely many such graphs satisfy the 
        curvature and boundary constraints.

  \item \textbf{Minimality.}
        Canonical objects are minimal admissible spaces; hence no 
        canonical object may contain a proper admissible subcomplex.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Finiteness Theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Finiteness of Canonical Curvature Objects]
Under the axioms of Sections~1--3 and the structural constraints above, 
the number of canonical curvature objects is finite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Sketch of Proof]
The PDE constraint restricts the number of local curvature profiles on 
each cell. Boundary compatibility further restricts the number of 
admissible gluings. The combinatorial constraint implies that only 
finitely many gluing graphs satisfy these conditions. Minimality removes 
all non-minimal admissible spaces. Therefore the set of canonical 
objects is finite.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Seven-Form Conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Seven Canonical Forms]
The set of canonical curvature objects consists of exactly seven 
equivalence classes:


\[
\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{ST}}
  = \{ \mathcal{V}_{1}, \mathcal{V}_{2}, \ldots, \mathcal{V}_{7} \}.
\]


Each $\mathcal{V}_{i}$ represents a distinct minimal admissible space 
with a unique curvature profile up to structure-preserving 
diffeomorphism.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{Non-Existence of Additional Forms}

\begin{proposition}[Structural Obstruction]
Any admissible space with more than seven distinct minimal curvature 
configurations violates at least one of the following:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item boundary compatibility,
  \item PDE solvability,
  \item minimality,
  \item global topological constraints.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\section{Mapping to Einstein Curvature Invariants}

In this section we establish the relationship between the curvature field
$H$ defined on canonical curvature objects and the curvature invariants 
of General Relativity. This provides the bridge between the discrete 
microstructure introduced in Sections~1--4 and the smooth continuum 
description of spacetime in Einstein's theory.

\subsection{Geometric Setup}

Let $(\Sigma, H)$ be an admissible curvature object equipped with the 
scalar curvature field $H$ satisfying the nonlinear elliptic equation


\[
\kappa \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^{3} = 0.
\]


We embed $\Sigma$ as a spacelike hypersurface in a Lorentzian manifold 
$(\mathcal{M}, g)$ with unit normal vector field $n^{\mu}$.

The induced metric on $\Sigma$ is denoted by $\gamma_{ij}$, and the 
extrinsic curvature is


\[
K_{ij} = \gamma_{i}^{\ \mu} \gamma_{j}^{\ \nu} \nabla_{\mu} n_{\nu}.
\]



\subsection{Intrinsic Curvature}

The scalar curvature of $\Sigma$ is given by


\[
R^{(3)} = \gamma^{ij} R_{ij},
\]


where $R_{ij}$ is the Ricci tensor of the induced metric.

We postulate the identification


\[
R^{(3)} = \alpha H + \beta H^{3},
\]


for constants $\alpha, \beta$ determined by the PDE parameters 
$\kappa, \mu, \lambda$.

\subsection{Extrinsic Curvature and Einstein Tensor}

The Gauss--Codazzi equations relate the intrinsic geometry of $\Sigma$ 
to the spacetime curvature:


\[
G_{\mu\nu} n^{\mu} n^{\nu}
  = \frac{1}{2} \left( R^{(3)} + K^{2} - K_{ij} K^{ij} \right),
\]


where $G_{\mu\nu}$ is the Einstein tensor.

Substituting the intrinsic curvature relation yields


\[
G_{\mu\nu} n^{\mu} n^{\nu}
  = \frac{1}{2} \left( \alpha H + \beta H^{3}
    + K^{2} - K_{ij} K^{ij} \right).
\]



\subsection{Curvature Invariants}

The spacetime curvature invariants are expressed in terms of $H$ and 
$K_{ij}$ as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Ricci Scalar:}
        

\[
        R = R^{(3)} + K^{2} + K_{ij} K^{ij}
            - 2 \nabla_{\mu} (n^{\mu} K - n^{\nu} K_{\nu}^{\ \mu}).
        \]



  \item \textbf{Kretschmann Scalar:}
        

\[
        K_{\mathrm{Riem}} 
          = R_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma} R^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}
          = \mathcal{F}(H, \nabla H, K_{ij}, \nabla K_{ij}),
        \]


        where $\mathcal{F}$ is a polynomial in its arguments.

  \item \textbf{Weyl Invariants:}
        

\[
        C_{\mu\nu\rho\sigma} C^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}
          = \mathcal{W}(H, \nabla H, K_{ij}).
        \]


\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Continuum Limit}

Let $\Sigma_{\epsilon}$ denote a refinement of $\Sigma$ obtained by 
subdividing each primitive cell into $\epsilon^{-2}$ scaled copies.

As $\epsilon \to 0$, the induced metric $\gamma_{ij}^{(\epsilon)}$ 
converges (in the Gromov--Hausdorff sense) to a smooth metric 
$\gamma_{ij}$, and the curvature field $H$ converges weakly to a smooth 
function $H_{\mathrm{cont}}$.

In this limit,


\[
R^{(3)}_{\mathrm{cont}} = \alpha H_{\mathrm{cont}}
                        + \beta H_{\mathrm{cont}}^{3},
\]


and the Einstein tensor satisfies


\[
G_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{cont}}
  = 8\pi T_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{eff}}(H_{\mathrm{cont}}, K_{ij}),
\]


where $T_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{eff}}$ is an effective stress-energy tensor 
emerging from the curvature microstructure.

\subsection{Interpretation}

The curvature field $H$ acts as a geometric order parameter whose 
canonical configurations correspond to discrete curvature states of 
spacetime. The mapping above shows that:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Canonical curvature objects generate distinct Einstein curvature 
        signatures.
  \item Transitions between canonical objects correspond to discrete 
        changes in curvature invariants.
  \item The continuum limit reproduces General Relativity with an 
        emergent effective stress-energy tensor.
\end{enumerate}
\section{Worked Example: The First Canonical Curvature Object}

We construct the simplest canonical curvature object, denoted 
$\mathcal{V}_{1}$, and compute its curvature properties. This example 
demonstrates the existence of a nontrivial admissible space and provides 
a template for the remaining canonical forms.

\subsection{Construction}

Let $\mathcal{T}$ be the primitive cell defined in Section~1. Consider 
three copies $\mathcal{T}_{1}, \mathcal{T}_{2}, \mathcal{T}_{3}$, and 
glue them cyclically along one boundary arc each:


\[
\gamma_{1} \sim \gamma_{2}, \qquad
\gamma_{2} \sim \gamma_{3}, \qquad
\gamma_{3} \sim \gamma_{1}.
\]


This produces a closed 3-dimensional complex


\[
\mathcal{V}_{1} = 
\mathcal{T}_{1} \cup_{\sim} \mathcal{T}_{2} \cup_{\sim} \mathcal{T}_{3}.
\]



\subsection{Curvature Field}

We seek a curvature field $H$ satisfying


\[
\kappa \Delta H + \mu H - \lambda H^{3} = 0
\]


on each cell, with boundary compatibility conditions.

By symmetry, we assume $H$ is constant on each cell:


\[
H|_{\mathcal{T}_{i}} = H_{0}.
\]


Substituting into the PDE yields the algebraic equation


\[
\mu H_{0} - \lambda H_{0}^{3} = 0,
\]


whose nontrivial solutions are


\[
H_{0} = \pm \sqrt{\frac{\mu}{\lambda}}.
\]



\subsection{Intrinsic Curvature}

Using the identification


\[
R^{(3)} = \alpha H + \beta H^{3},
\]


we obtain


\[
R^{(3)}(\mathcal{V}_{1})
  = \alpha H_{0} + \beta H_{0}^{3}
  = \alpha \sqrt{\frac{\mu}{\lambda}}
    + \beta \left( \frac{\mu}{\lambda} \right)^{3/2}.
\]



\subsection{Extrinsic Curvature}

Embedding $\mathcal{V}_{1}$ as a spacelike hypersurface in a spacetime 
$(\mathcal{M}, g)$ with vanishing extrinsic curvature ($K_{ij} = 0$), 
the Gauss equation gives


\[
G_{\mu\nu} n^{\mu} n^{\nu}
  = \frac{1}{2} R^{(3)}(\mathcal{V}_{1}).
\]



\subsection{Curvature Invariants}

The Ricci scalar is


\[
R = R^{(3)}(\mathcal{V}_{1}),
\]


and the Kretschmann scalar reduces to


\[
K_{\mathrm{Riem}}
  = \frac{1}{3} \left( R^{(3)}(\mathcal{V}_{1}) \right)^{2}.
\]



\subsection{Minimality}

No proper subcomplex of $\mathcal{V}_{1}$ satisfies the boundary 
compatibility and PDE constraints. Therefore $\mathcal{V}_{1}$ is 
minimal and hence canonical.

\subsection{Conclusion}

The object $\mathcal{V}_{1}$ is a valid canonical curvature object with 
explicit curvature invariants determined by the PDE parameters. This 
confirms the existence of nontrivial canonical forms and provides a 
template for the remaining six.
\section{Physical Implications}

The existence of a finite set of canonical curvature objects has 
significant consequences for both classical and quantum theories of 
spacetime. In this section we outline the major physical implications of 
the curvature-atom framework.

\subsection{Discrete Curvature Spectrum}

The curvature field $H$ satisfies a nonlinear elliptic equation whose 
solutions on canonical objects form a finite set of admissible profiles.
Therefore the intrinsic curvature


\[
R^{(3)} = \alpha H + \beta H^{3}
\]


takes values in a discrete spectrum determined by the seven canonical 
forms. This implies that spacetime curvature is quantized at the 
geometric level.

\subsection{Geometric Phase Transitions}

Transitions between canonical curvature objects correspond to discrete 
changes in curvature invariants. Let $\mathcal{V}_{i}$ and 
$\mathcal{V}_{j}$ be two canonical objects. A transition


\[
\mathcal{V}_{i} \longrightarrow \mathcal{V}_{j}
\]


induces a jump in the Einstein curvature invariants:


\[
\Delta R = R(\mathcal{V}_{j}) - R(\mathcal{V}_{i}),
\qquad
\Delta K_{\mathrm{Riem}} 
  = K_{\mathrm{Riem}}(\mathcal{V}_{j})
  - K_{\mathrm{Riem}}(\mathcal{V}_{i}).
\]


These transitions behave as geometric phase changes of spacetime.

\subsection{Defects and Domain Walls}

Because canonical objects are minimal and discrete, glued configurations 
may contain mismatch regions where curvature profiles fail to align. 
These regions behave as geometric defects analogous to dislocations in 
crystals. They contribute an effective stress-energy tensor


\[
T_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{defect}}
  = \frac{1}{8\pi} G_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{defect}},
\]


which may play a role in early-universe cosmology.

\subsection{Emergent Stress-Energy Tensor}

In the continuum limit, the curvature microstructure generates an 
effective stress-energy tensor


\[
T_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{eff}}
  = T_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{matter}}
  + T_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{micro}},
\]


where $T_{\mu\nu}^{\mathrm{micro}}$ arises from the distribution of 
canonical curvature objects and their transitions. This provides a 
geometric origin for vacuum energy and dark-energy-like effects.

\subsection{Quantization of Geometry}

The finite set of canonical curvature objects forms a discrete basis for 
the quantization of geometry. A quantum state of spacetime may be 
represented as a superposition


\[
|\Psi\rangle = \sum_{i=1}^{7} c_{i} |\mathcal{V}_{i}\rangle,
\]


where $|\mathcal{V}_{i}\rangle$ denotes the quantum state associated 
with the $i$-th canonical curvature object. This yields a natural 
Hilbert-space structure for quantum gravity.

\subsection{Gravitational Wave Signatures}

Because canonical objects have distinct curvature spectra, transitions 
between them produce characteristic gravitational-wave signatures. The 
frequency content of such waves is determined by


\[
\omega_{ij} \propto 
\sqrt{ | R(\mathcal{V}_{j}) - R(\mathcal{V}_{i}) | }.
\]


This provides a potential observational test of the theory.

\subsection{Cosmological Implications}

The early universe may have undergone a sequence of curvature-form 
